For `DATABASE`, `object_name` must equal `database_name`. For `COLUMN`, `object_name` identifies the relation. For
`CONSTRAINT`, `object_name` is the table and `constraint_name` the primary key, unique, or foreign key constraint on
it; `redshift_constraints` lists the names. An empty `text` clears the comment using `IS NULL`.

## Lifecycle and Ownership

The SQL caller must own the object or be a superuser. Reads use the target database's `pg_description` and object
catalogs; constraint comments are read from `pg_description` joined to `pg_constraint`. Database comments execute in the
database being annotated, as required by Redshift. Shared database objects, external table/column comments, and columns
of late-binding views are not supported.

Creation sets the annotation on an existing object; it does not create the object. Destroy clears only the comment and
leaves the target intact. It does not restore any annotation overwritten during adoption. Do not manage the same
annotation with another Terraform resource or a model/migration tool.

## Reconciliation

Refresh reads the comment text from `pg_description`; create and update read it again, run one `COMMENT ON ... IS`
statement only when the catalog text differs from `text`, and re-read the catalog to verify the result; every other
change replaces the comment: `COMMENT ON ... IS NULL` clears the old target's comment, and the new target is annotated.

| Change                                                                                            | Result                                                                                                                   |
|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `database_name`, `object_type`, `object_name`, `schema_name`, `column_name`, or `constraint_name` | replaces the comment: the old target's comment is cleared, the new target's is set, and neither object changes otherwise |
| `text` changed to a nonempty value                                                                | `COMMENT ON ... IS 'text'`                                                                                               |
| `text` changed to `""`                                                                            | `COMMENT ON ... IS NULL`                                                                                                 |

**Drift.** Refresh compares the catalog text with the state, so text edited or removed outside Terraform shows in the
next plan and is set back with `COMMENT ON`; a missing annotation reads as an empty `text`. A target object or database
that no longer exists removes the resource from state; the next plan creates it again, and that create fails until the
object exists.

